$DIR/main.p:17:10: the value receiver of Get copies total, which holds a sync.Mutex: a lock may not be copied, so pass a pointer or keep the value in one place
    A value that holds a lock is copied out of a variable, field, element or
    dereference. A sync.Mutex, sync.RWMutex, sync.Once, sync.WaitGroup or
    sync.Cond, and outside pluto/rt an rt.Lock, may not be copied once it is in
    use: the copy starts with the state the lock had at that moment, such as
    locked or with waiters, and the two copies then guard nothing together. The
    compiler cannot tell the first use, so every copy from a place of a value
    whose type holds one, directly or through fields and array elements, is an
    error: an assignment or initialization, an argument, a result, a value sent
    on a channel, a range value, a value receiver and a conversion, including to
    an interface.
    A composite literal, a zero value and the result of a call are new values,
    and moving an owner is a move, so none of them is a copy. Pass a pointer to
    the value, give the method a pointer receiver, or range over the indexes and
    take each element's address.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3505" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:17:23: the value receiver of Get copies p, which holds a sync.Mutex: a lock may not be copied, so pass a pointer or keep the value in one place
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E3505" for more)
